#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183921 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "563087c2-8d87-5b95-833d-f9892d9276a6" :question/math-academy-id "e-10535" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to 0} \\dfrac{\\arccos(x) -\\pi}{x-\\pi}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y=\\arccos(x){:}$\n\n![](images/9d/9dcc5a116c2a564547283783a7df7b1cd3a9ab3b76ac24dc2695c4cb367796d5.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x)$ is $x \\in [-1,1].$  \n\nNow, substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)-\\pi }{x-\\pi }) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)-\\pi }{0-\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\pi )}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(-\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183922 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184239 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcc5d54-e7fd-5be5-9d00-4240d03e21cb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Limits at Infinity"}] :question/id #uuid "c1ba0b9f-78d6-5791-8816-b71da089e57f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10536" :question/problem "What is the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y = \\arccos(x-1){:}$\n\n![](images/4c/4ccf7e79e19f49027f615a075e655139e6926b3ba30fe35d63078fd7917d8869.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\arccos(x-1)$ is $x \\in [0,2].$ This means that $\\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty} \\arccos(x-1)$ is undefined."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183923 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184240 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a58f115-f368-5171-af73-37084085b42a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Limit Involving Trigonometric Functions and Their Inverses"}] :question/id #uuid "7ce46ca9-cfff-5e8b-89b9-a2a7b34921fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-10537" :question/problem "Compute the value of $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\, \\infty}\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2}{3}\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right)\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of  $y =\\arctan \\left(2x+1\\right){:}$  \n\n![](images/06/06d01e3eb47f69e5172e0ba3c99708a05c79b12a48764fe29c50e94f93b73dd1.png)\n\nThe domain of   $\\arctan \\left(2x+1 \\right)$ is $(-\\infty,\\infty).$   \n\nAs $x$ increases to $\\infty,$ the graph tends to $y={\\color{blue}\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x+1)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184238 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f458c61-bc51-5653-9ecb-1e96eb5c335e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Limit of an Inverse Trigonometric Function at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38842a19-d74b-54c4-a974-cb084d4878b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504131} {:answer/id #uuid "5b547071-324e-5160-9dc0-f0040f86732b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504192} {:answer/id #uuid "722b4436-9d5a-54e4-828b-0192b0943a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504230} {:answer/id #uuid "7c76e33c-6ae4-5f24-a69d-dd346ae84b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Undefined" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186504250} {:answer/id #uuid "9e0e2823-4c04-5141-8a5f-03139